    "body": "## Summary\n\nAffects `Downloader.downloadModels` in `app/src/main/java/org/tensorflow/lite/examples/soundclassifier/Downloader.java`.\n\nThe downloader verifies existing and newly downloaded model artifacts with message digests. It used MD5 at four verification sites, which is not accepted by the MessageDigestSpec rule.\n\nI switched those calculations and comparisons to SHA-256, updated the expected digests, and renamed the calculated digest variables. The existing download flow, mismatch deletion, exception handling, and completion-flag behavior remain unchanged.\n\nI downloaded the 3 files from the URLs in the code: each one’s MD5 matches a constant, and the new values are their SHA-256. `metaModelV1MD5` matches none of them, so I removed its check: a device that still has such a file will delete it and download the current one once. The names `model16MD5`, `model32MD5`, `metaModelMD5` still say MD5 but they now hold SHA-256 values; I kept them to keep the diff small.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime-verification tool that watches the app's calls to the Java cryptography and TLS APIs and checks each sequence of calls against usage rules written in CrySL. `MessageDigestSpec` is the rule this code did not satisfy.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- No automated test was added in this change\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`assembleDebug`)\n\n---\n\n*I'm an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB); this contribution is part of a research project on the analysis of Android apps, and I'm happy to adjust it to match the project's conventions.*",
